[PhilidorParent "Method of giving Check-mate with a Rook and a Bishop against a Rook"]
[PhilidorBranchMove "4"]

1. Rc8+ {[from the parent game] The rook gives check.} Rd8 {[from the parent game] The rook
covers the check.} 2. Rc7 {[from the parent game] The rook, at the adverse queen's bishop's
second square.} Rd2 {[from the parent game] The rook, at the adverse queen's second square. (b)}
3. Rb7 {[from the parent game] The rook, at the adverse queen's knight's second square. (c)} Rd1
{[from the parent game] The rook, at the adverse queen's square. (d)} 4. Rg7 {The rook, at the
adverse king's knight's second square.} Kf8 {The king, at his bishop's square.} 5. Rh7 {The
rook, at the adverse king's rook's second square. (a) NOTE: You force him, by this move, to play
his rook to your king's knight's square, to ward off the mate; which gives you the means of
taking his rook for nothing.} Rg1 {The rook, at the adverse king's knight's square.} 6. Rc7 {The
rook, at the adverse queen's bishop's second square. (b) NOTE: The only way to ward off the mate
is, to play his king to his knight's square; for, if he had given you check, you would have
preserved all the strength of your attack, by covering with your bishop.} Kg8 {The king, at his
knight's square.} 7. Rc8+ {The rook gives check at the adverse queen's bishop's square.} Kh7
{The king, at his rook's second square.} 8. Rh8+ {The rook gives check, at the adverse king's
rook's square, and must win.} *
